CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ names a later non-throwing member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ names a later throwing member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
W<int>::a() was taken as non-throwing: the kept noexcept-specifier was
evaluated again for the class template specialization without `this', so
`b()' did not call W<int>::b.  Binding `this' there evaluated every
member's specifier eagerly, which instantiated unused templates and broke
cpp20_ranges_basic.  The specifier is now evaluated when it is needed
([except.spec]/12): by the noexcept operator for a callee, and for the
defined functions at the end of type checking, in the class's scope, with
its template arguments and with `this'.
